LLVM's C++ runtimes - libc++, libc++abi and libunwind - LLVM
itself, Clang and lld, as they are upstream, with TheTaaJ's changes on top (see the
history of this directory).

  from     https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project
  release  llvmorg-18.1.3 (llvm-project-18.1.3.src.tar.xz,
           sha256 2929f62d69dec0379e529eb632c40e15191e36f3bd58c2cb2df0413a0dc48651)

18.1.3 because it is the clang that builds TheTaaJ (Ubuntu's clang 18):
libc++'s headers are written for a clang of their own release or newer.

Of each runtime only include/ and src/ are here (and libcxx/vendor, the
default assertion handler), with its licence - not
the tests, benchmarks, documentation or build system around them (their
CMakeLists.txt are kept, as the lists of what the libraries are made
of).

Of LLVM (llvm/): include/, lib/ with only the two targets TheTaaJ runs
on (lib/Target/X86 and lib/Target/AArch64, and the files common to all
targets in lib/Target), utils/TableGen (the source of llvm-tblgen), the
two tools built so far (tools/llc and tools/llvm-mc) and the licence.
Of the other targets only RISC-V's .td files (lib/Target/RISCV, no C++):
the TargetParser library, which every LLVM program has in it, is
written by llvm-tblgen from RISCV.td whatever the targets. Not LLVM's
tests (780 MB), documentation, examples, the other tools or the other
targets.

Of Clang (clang/): include/, the libraries the clang program is made
of (lib/: Basic, Lex, Parse, AST, ASTMatchers, Sema, Analysis, Edit,
APINotes, Support, Serialization, Driver, Frontend, Frontend/Rewrite,
FrontendTool, CodeGen, ExtractAPI, Index, Format, Rewrite,
Tooling/Core, Tooling/Inclusions), lib/Headers (the headers clang
brings with it: stddef.h, stdarg.h, the intrinsics), tools/driver (the
program), utils/TableGen (the source of clang-tblgen), its top
CMakeLists.txt (the version) and its licence. Not Clang's tests,
documentation, the static analyser, the ARC migrator, the interpreter
or the other tools.

Of lld (lld/): include/, Common/, ELF/ (the linker for ELF files, the
only kind TheTaaJ has), tools/lld (the program), its top CMakeLists.txt
(the version) and its licence. Not lld's tests, documentation, or its
linkers for Windows (COFF, MinGW), Apple (MachO) or WebAssembly.

LLVM is not built with CMake here but by tools/build-llvm.py, which
reads the same CMakeLists.txt for its lists of sources. The settings
CMake would have found out by trying the C library are in
taaj/llvm-config.txt.

